OBJCOPY(1)                User Commands                 OBJCOPY(1)

NAME
    objcopy - copy and transform ELF files

SYNOPSIS
    objcopy [options] infile [outfile]

DESCRIPTION
    objcopy copies an ELF64 file, or every ELF member of an archive, and
    applies the options on the way. Without outfile the program replaces
    infile. The input files are relocatable objects, executables and
    shared objects of x86_64 and aarch64 in little-endian format.

    An executable or a shared object retains its program headers and the
    file offsets of all allocated sections. The loaded image does not
    change. The section header table and the sections without the
    allocated flag follow the allocated contents. A relocatable object
    receives a new layout. The symbol table is numbered again, and every
    relocation section receives the new symbol indices. Section indices
    in the headers and in groups follow the sections that remain.

    A relocation section belongs to its target section. It disappears with
    the target section. Symbols that a relocation uses remain, except
    with -S. A relocation against a symbol in a removed section ends the
    program with the message "symbol required but not present".

OPTIONS
    -I bfdname, --input-target=bfdname
        Set the format of the input. The values are elf64-x86-64,
        elf64-littleaarch64, elf64-little and default.
    -O bfdname, --output-target=bfdname
        Set the format of the output. The value binary writes the
        loadable contents as a raw image. The other values write an ELF
        file and must match the machine of the input.
    -j name, --only-section=name
        Copy only the sections with this name. The option is repeatable.
    -R name, --remove-section=name
        Remove the sections with this name. The option is repeatable.
    -S, --strip-all
        Remove all symbols and the debug sections. Relocations against
        removed symbols disappear.
    -g, --strip-debug
        Remove the debug sections, the file symbols and the section
        symbols that no relocation uses.
    --strip-unneeded
        Remove the debug sections and all symbols that no relocation or
        dynamic link needs.
    -N name, --strip-symbol=name
        Remove the symbols with this name unless a relocation uses them.
    -K name, --keep-symbol=name
        Do not remove the symbols with this name when another option
        removes them.
    --add-section name=file
        Add a section with the contents of file. The section has no flags.
    -p, --preserve-dates
        Give the output file the access and modification times of the
        input file.

    The arguments of -j, -R, -N and -K accept the wildcards * and ?.

BINARY OUTPUT
    The output format binary writes the sections that have the allocated
    flag and contents in the file. Each section lies at the distance of
    its load address from the lowest load address of the written sections.
    Gaps contain zero bytes. The program derives a load address from the
    physical address of the loadable segment that contains the section.

DIFFERENCES FROM GNU
    The removal or selection of allocated sections of an executable does
    not change the segments. GNU objcopy changes them. The program
    accepts the formats of x86_64 and aarch64 only. It reads no input in
    the format binary. Sections with extended indices (more than 65279
    sections) are not supported.

EXAMPLES
    objcopy --strip-debug prog prog.small
    objcopy -O binary kernel.elf kernel.bin
    objcopy --add-section .note.build=build.txt prog
    objcopy -R .comment -R '.note*' libx.so

SEE ALSO
    strip(1), readelf(1), ar(1), nm(1)
